Split out of #176 (docs fix for the ephemeral agentname), which is the docs half of the same symptom. This is the behavioural half and was deliberately left out of that ticket.

App::karr::Role::ClaimTimeout::check_claim allows a mutation once the existing claim is older than claim_timeout — by design (an expired claim must not block anybody, and karr pick reaps them). But the takeover is completely silent, and handoff/move then overwrite claimed_by with the new name, so the previous holder leaves no trace on the card at all.

Reproduced in a temp repo (claim_timeout: 1s):

    A=$(karr agentname); karr move 1 in-progress --claim "$A"
    # ... claim expires ...
    B=$(karr agentname)
    karr handoff 1 --claim "$B" --note x
    -> "Handed off task 1 -> review"   exit 0
    karr show 1 -> Claimed:  <B>

Nothing on stdout or stderr says a claim changed hands. While the claim is still live the same mismatch is refused loudly and the error names the holder ("Task 1 is claimed by <A>"), which is the recovery route #176 now documents; after expiry that signal disappears entirely, exactly when it would be most useful — an agent that lost its own name gets a success instead of a hint.

It matters beyond the confused agent: karr-foundation attributes stalls per claim name, and a silent re-stamp moves that attribution to a name nobody is holding, with no record that the card was ever held by someone else.

Suggested shape (small, one place): when check_claim proceeds only because the claim expired AND the incoming claimant differs from the holder, emit one line to STDERR, e.g.
    karr: task 1: taking over expired claim held by <A>
Return value and exit code unchanged, so nothing that currently succeeds starts failing. The activity log already records the acting claim name; the point is making the handover visible at the moment it happens.

Care needed: check_claim is shared by move/edit/delete/archive/handoff, and delete/archive pass undef as claimant (they refuse any live claim rather than comparing names), so "differs from the holder" has to be defined for the undef case too — probably "warn whenever an expired claim was stepped over", regardless of claimant. Decide that deliberately; it changes stderr for five commands and a few tests assert clean stderr.
